<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
The legacy <code>let { body = …; }</code> form is a deprecated Nix syntax
that evaluates to the value of the <code>body</code> attribute. It is kept
only for backwards compatibility, and modern Nix gates it behind extra
experimental features.
</p>
<p>
Using the modern <code>let … in …</code> form is clearer, idiomatic, and more
portable across current Nix tooling and versions.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Rewrite legacy <code>let { body = expr; ... }</code> expressions as
<code>let ... in expr</code>. If you actually want an attribute set value,
use a plain <code>{ ... }</code> attrset instead of a legacy <code>let</code>.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Unsafe — deprecated legacy <code>let { body = …; }</code> syntax:</p>
<sample language="nix">
let { body = 1; }
</sample>
<p>Unsafe — legacy syntax with additional bindings:</p>
<sample language="nix">
let {
  body = a;
  a = 1;
}
</sample>
<p>Safe — modern <code>let … in …</code> syntax:</p>
<sample language="nix">
let
  x = 1;
in
x
</sample>
<p>Safe — if you mean to return an attribute set, use a plain attrset:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ a = 1; }
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>
Nix Reference Manual:
<a href="https://nix.dev/manual/nix/latest/language/syntax.html">syntax</a>
</li>
<li>
CWE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/477.html">CWE-477:
Use of Obsolete Function</a>
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
